<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andersen Air Force Base</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andersen Air Force Base is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Air Force base in the north of Guam, built after American forces retook the island from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1944. It served as a base for B-29 raids on Japan in 1945 and for B-52 bombers in the Vietnam War, and remains one of the main US military installations in the western Pacific.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andersen-air-force-base.jpg" alt="Andersen Air Force Base" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tech. Sgt. Richard Ebensberger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dyess_B-1s_deploy_to_Andersen,_take_over_Continuous_Bomber_Presence_operations_170206-F-LP948-046.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The base lies mainly within the village of Yigo, at the northern end of Guam, about 48 km from Naval Base Guam at the other end of the island; since 2009 both have been managed by Joint Region Marianas. It is named for Brigadier General James Roy Andersen, who died on 26 February 1945 when his B-24 Liberator crashed between Kwajalein and Johnston Island. Guam's almost unrestricted airspace and the bombing range on Farallon de Medinilla, 296 km to the north, make it a training ground, and it is the only base in the western Pacific that can permanently service heavy strategic bombers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> seized Guam in December 1941, and the 3rd Amphibious Corps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">United States Marine Corps</a> landed on 21 July 1944. North Field, the first air base built after the island was retaken, was begun in November 1944 with the help of Navy Seabees; with Northwest Field it had four main runways and revetments for over 200 B-29s, about 2,400 km from Tokyo. The 314th Bombardment Wing arrived on 16 January 1945, flying daylight raids on Japanese factories and refineries and, from March 1945, low-level night incendiary raids. After the war its B-29s dropped supplies to Allied prisoners. The base took the name Andersen on 7 October 1949.</p><p>B-29s flew from it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1950, and Strategic Air Command rotated B-36, B-47 and B-50 bombers through it. The first B-52 arrived in March 1964, and from June 1965 Operation Arc Light sent bombers over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> until 1973. In Operation Linebacker II, between 18 and 29 December 1972, more than 150 B-52s flew 729 sorties. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in 1975 the base received almost 40,000 refugees and processed another 109,000 for passage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Control passed from Strategic Air Command to Pacific Air Forces in 1989, and the host unit has been the 36th Wing, a non-flying wing that supports deployed forces. In 1991 the base cared for evacuees of the Mount Pinatubo eruption and became home to the Thirteenth Air Force. Bombers kept a continuous presence there from 2004 until April 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andersen Air Force Base” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It remains an unincorporated territory of the United States, dependent on military bases such as <b>Andersen Air Force Base</b> and on tourism, with continuing debate over its political status.</blockquote><a href="/state/guam">Guam</a></li></ul></section>
